{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"WARNING: RISKS FROM CONCOMITANT USE WITH OPIOIDS; ABUSE, MISUSE, AND ADDICTION and DEPENDENCE AND WITHDRAWAL REACTIONS ... FDA/DailyMed SPL ... url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=12527569-8eb3-4624-b3c0-5c0e0c5f88c9","fda_source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-requiring-boxed-warning-updated-improve-safe-use-benzodiazepine-drug-class"],"proposed_value":"WARNING: RISKS FROM CONCOMITANT USE WITH OPIOIDS; ABUSE, MISUSE, AND ADDICTION; AND DEPENDENCE AND WITHDRAWAL REACTIONS. Boxed warning present for diazepam benzodiazepine products: opioid co-use can cause profound sedation, respiratory depression, coma, and death; benzodiazepine use exposes users to abuse, misuse, addiction, overdose, and death; use more frequently than recommended can cause physical dependence and potentially life-threatening withdrawal reactions.","rationale":"Current source is DailyMed, which is not permissible for the black-box field. FDA accessdata VALTOCO and LIBERVANT labels confirm a boxed warning, but the CSV text is duplicated and product-specific to LIBERVANT; the broader current FDA diazepam rescue product label wording is better represented by a concise FDA-sourced warning summary.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"diazepam","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"diazepam","rationale":"FDA labels and NCBI sources identify diazepam as the active ingredient/generic name.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No separate alternate generic name was found; important aliases are trade names/formulation terms rather than different generic names.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"Valium; Diastat; Diastat AcuDial; Valtoco; Libervant; diazepam rectal gel; diazepam nasal spray; diazepam buccal film; diazepam auto-injector","rationale":"Search aliases are missing. Several RCTs and FDA labels use formulation/trade-name terms such as Diastat, VALTOCO, LIBERVANT, and diazepam auto-injector.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24111974/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"FDA-approved US labels/records exist for diazepam rescue products including VALTOCO and LIBERVANT; FDA also documents earlier diazepam approvals.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=382512","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS: somnolence 23%, neurologic: ataxia 15%, CNS: dizziness 12%, respiratory: nasal discomfort 6% for nasal product, GI: nausea 5%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: somnolence 23%; CNS: headache 5%; GI: diarrhea 4%; neurologic: ataxia 3%; CNS: dizziness 3%; respiratory/local: nasal discomfort 5% for nasal spray","rationale":"The current ataxia 15%, dizziness 12%, nasal discomfort 6%, and nausea 5% values were not supported by the FDA VALTOCO/LIBERVANT label tables reviewed. FDA label data support rectal-gel trial rates of somnolence 23%, headache 5%, diarrhea 4%, ataxia 3%, dizziness 3%, and VALTOCO local nasal discomfort 5%.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Not a CYP inducer/inhibitor; CYP3A4/CYP2C19 substrate","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Not a major CYP inducer/inhibitor; primarily metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4","rationale":"FDA labeling and NCBI Medical Genetics Summaries support CYP2C19/CYP3A4 metabolism. The current wording is directionally correct, but 'substrate' is better stated as metabolized by these enzymes.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK379740/"]},{"current_value":"Seizure clusters / rescue; Status epilepticus","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Seizure clusters / rescue; Status epilepticus","rationale":"VALTOCO and LIBERVANT labels support seizure-cluster/rescue use. NCBI LiverTox/Bookshelf supports IV diazepam use for status epilepticus or severe recurrent seizures.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; Wikipedia anticonvulsant drug-class list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata prescribing information and approval records; NCBI Bookshelf/LiverTox; Epilepsy Foundation (epilepsy.com); PubMed RCT records; ClinicalTrials.gov records","rationale":"Wikipedia and Epilepsy Foundation Australia are not in the trusted-source list for this audit, and DailyMed is not permissible for black-box verification. Replace with trusted FDA, NCBI, epilepsy.com, PubMed, and ClinicalTrials.gov sources.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/","https://www.epilepsy.com/treatment/seizure-rescue-therapies/oral-rescue-medicines","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00319501"]},{"current_value":"Tablet; oral solution; rectal gel; nasal spray; IV injection; autoinjector in some settings","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Tablet; oral solution; rectal gel; nasal spray; IV/IM injection; buccal film","rationale":"FDA/NCBI sources support tablets, oral solution, rectal gel, nasal spray, injection, and LIBERVANT buccal film. Diazepam auto-injector is supported as an investigational/clinical-trial formulation, not as a current approved/available formulation in the reviewed FDA sources.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24111974/"]},{"current_value":"Diazepam 20-50 h; active metabolites up to ~100 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"Diazepam half-life is formulation/population dependent: about 49.2 h after a 10 mg VALTOCO dose, about 15-100 h across adult age range after IV dosing, and desmethyldiazepam about 147 h in LIBERVANT labeling","rationale":"Current text is partly supported for typical diazepam half-life, but FDA labels document a wider age-related diazepam range and a desmethyldiazepam half-life around 147 h, which contradicts 'active metabolites up to ~100 h'.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Liver","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver","rationale":"FDA labeling states diazepam metabolism is primarily hepatic and involves CYP2C19/CYP3A4 pathways.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Rescue dosing individualized by age/weight/product","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Rescue dosing individualized by age/weight/product","rationale":"VALTOCO and LIBERVANT dosing is age/weight/product specific; current summary is acceptable.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Rescue dosing individualized by age/weight/product","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"Product-specific rescue dosing; for VALTOCO/LIBERVANT, do not use more than 2 doses per episode, more than 1 episode every 5 days, or more than 5 episodes per month","rationale":"Current value is directionally correct but less specific than FDA labeling for approved seizure-cluster rescue products.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Precise antiseizure mechanism is unknown; diazepam enhances GABA-A receptor-mediated chloride-channel inhibition by increasing GABA effects at the receptor.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Precise antiseizure mechanism is unknown; diazepam potentiates GABAergic neurotransmission through binding at the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports that the exact mechanism is not fully understood and is thought to involve potentiation of GABAergic neurotransmission at the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A receptors.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata labeling","rationale":"DailyMed is not in the trusted source list supplied for this audit; FDA/accessdata labels support the mechanism statement.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No clinically meaningful QT effect established","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No change proposed without a specific trusted QT source","rationale":"No cited row source specifically supports this QT statement. Absence of a QT warning in reviewed labels is not strong enough to verify the cell as written.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Diastat; Diastat AcuDial; Libervant; Valtoco; Valium","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Diastat; Diastat AcuDial; Libervant; Valtoco; Valium","rationale":"Current trade names are supported. The deterministic suggestion to add 'Diazepam' as a trade name should not be applied because diazepam is the generic name, not a trade name.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/"]},{"current_value":"Rescue/status dosing is intermittent, not chronic daily dosing","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Rescue/status dosing is intermittent, not chronic daily dosing","rationale":"FDA labeling for seizure-cluster rescue products limits treatment frequency and does not recommend chronic daily use for seizure-cluster rescue dosing.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"1963","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"1963","rationale":"FDA review material for VALTOCO states diazepam oral tablet was approved by FDA on November 15, 1963; NCBI LiverTox also states US approval in 1963.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2020/211635Orig1s000MedR.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/"]}],"generic_name":"diazepam","missing_rcts":[{"pmid":"9637805","proposed_link_text":"Dreifuss1998|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/","title":"A comparison of rectal diazepam gel and placebo for acute repetitive seizures.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/","why_relevant":"Placebo-controlled randomized acute repetitive seizure trial of diazepam rectal gel. FDA labeling reports the corresponding 91-patient study and seizure-free rates of 62% vs 20%, which materially changes the seizure-freedom differential maximum to 42%."},{"pmid":"24111974","proposed_link_text":"AbouKhalil2013|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24111974/","title":"A double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled trial of a diazepam auto-injector administered by caregivers to patients with epilepsy who require intermittent intervention for acute repetitive seizures.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24111974/","why_relevant":"Phase III randomized double-blind placebo-controlled multicenter diazepam auto-injector trial for acute repetitive seizures; ClinicalTrials.gov NCT00319501 links this study to a completed Phase 3 trial."},{"pmid":"11547716","proposed_link_text":"Alldredge2001|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11547716/","title":"A comparison of lorazepam, diazepam, and placebo for the treatment of out-of-hospital status epilepticus.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11547716/","why_relevant":"Randomized placebo-controlled diazepam-arm trial for status epilepticus; ClinicalTrials.gov NCT00004297 identifies the study as Phase 3 randomized diazepam vs lorazepam vs placebo for prehospital status epilepticus."}],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"Reviewed diazepam rescue/febrile/stroke RCTs report time-to-next seizure, seizure frequency, seizure occurrence, or recurrence; RR50 is not a standard/extractable endpoint in the cited records.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8510706/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2202804/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"Median percent seizure-frequency change is not extractable from the cited diazepam placebo RCT abstracts/label summaries as a drug-minus-placebo MPC endpoint.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"1.8-21 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: vanTuijl2021 diazepam for 3 days after acute stroke 1.8%; Cereghino1998 single caregiver-administered rectal diazepam dose 21%; Autret1990 intermittent oral diazepam during fever 3.5%)","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"1.8-42% (drug minus placebo seizure-free or recurrence-free differential from placebo-controlled RCTs with extractable rates: vanTuijl2021 diazepam for 3 days after acute stroke 1.8%; Dreifuss1998 diazepam rectal gel acute repetitive seizure study 42%; Cereghino1998 single caregiver-administered rectal diazepam dose 21%; Autret1990 intermittent oral diazepam during fever 3.5%)","rationale":"The cited values for vanTuijl2021, Cereghino1998, and Autret1990 are supported by the respective abstract/label evidence. However, the current range omits a qualifying rectal diazepam gel placebo-controlled RCT summarized in FDA labeling with 62% vs 20% seizure-free patients, a 42% differential; therefore the maximum is not 21%.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2202804/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"vanTuijl2021|1.8|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/|784; Cereghino1998|21|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/|114; Autret1990|3.5|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2202804/|185","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"vanTuijl2021|1.8|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/|784; Dreifuss1998|42|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/|91; Cereghino1998|21|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/|114; Autret1990|3.5|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2202804/|185","rationale":"Add the missing Dreifuss/FDA-labeled 91-patient rectal diazepam placebo-controlled trial result, 62% vs 20% seizure-free.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]}],"overall_status":"needs_update","proposed_row_updates":[{"current_value":"CNS: somnolence 23%, neurologic: ataxia 15%, CNS: dizziness 12%, respiratory: nasal discomfort 6% for nasal product, GI: nausea 5%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: somnolence 23%; CNS: headache 5%; GI: diarrhea 4%; neurologic: ataxia 3%; CNS: dizziness 3%; respiratory/local: nasal discomfort 5% for nasal spray","reason":"FDA label percentages contradict several current adverse-event values.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; Wikipedia anticonvulsant drug-class list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata prescribing information and approval records; NCBI Bookshelf/LiverTox; Epilepsy Foundation (epilepsy.com); PubMed RCT records; ClinicalTrials.gov records","reason":"Replace untrusted/inapplicable sources with trusted sources specified by audit instructions.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/","https://www.epilepsy.com/treatment/seizure-rescue-therapies/oral-rescue-medicines","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00319501"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=boxed_warning_found; setid=12527569-8eb3-4624-b3c0-5c0e0c5f88c9; published=Nov 15, 2024; title=LIBERVANT (DIAZEPAM) FILM [AQUESTIVE THERAPEUTICS]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=12527569-8eb3-4624-b3c0-5c0e0c5f88c9","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata prescribing information; status=boxed_warning_found; Reference ID=5681158; title=VALTOCO (diazepam) nasal spray; url=https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","reason":"DailyMed is not permissible for black-box verification; FDA/accessdata label is permissible.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"05-20-2026","field":"fda_black_box_warning_verified","proposed_value":"05-20-2026","reason":"Verification should be tied to FDA/accessdata rather than DailyMed.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Tablet; oral solution; rectal gel; nasal spray; IV injection; autoinjector in some settings","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Tablet; oral solution; rectal gel; nasal spray; IV/IM injection; buccal film","reason":"Add FDA-approved buccal film and remove unsupported currently available auto-injector wording.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Diazepam 20-50 h; active metabolites up to ~100 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"Diazepam half-life is formulation/population dependent: about 49.2 h after a 10 mg VALTOCO dose, about 15-100 h across adult age range after IV dosing, and desmethyldiazepam about 147 h in LIBERVANT labeling","reason":"FDA labels support a broader range and longer desmethyldiazepam half-life than the current text.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata labeling","reason":"Use trusted FDA/accessdata source naming instead of DailyMed.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"Valium; Diastat; Diastat AcuDial; Valtoco; Libervant; diazepam rectal gel; diazepam nasal spray; diazepam buccal film; diazepam auto-injector","reason":"Alias/search field is empty despite important trade names and formulation names used in labels and RCT titles.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24111974/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"vanTuijl2021|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/; Cereghino1998|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/; Uhari1995|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7776115/; Rosman1993|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8510706/; Autret1990|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2202804/","field":"pubmed_phase_ii_iii_rct_links","proposed_value":"vanTuijl2021|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/; Cereghino1998|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/; Uhari1995|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7776115/; Rosman1993|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8510706/; Autret1990|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2202804/; Dreifuss1998|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/; AbouKhalil2013|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24111974/; Alldredge2001|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11547716/","reason":"Existing links are valid but incomplete; at least three relevant placebo-controlled diazepam seizure/status epilepticus RCTs are missing.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24111974/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11547716/","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00319501","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00004297"]},{"current_value":"1.8-21 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: vanTuijl2021 diazepam for 3 days after acute stroke 1.8%; Cereghino1998 single caregiver-administered rectal diazepam dose 21%; Autret1990 intermittent oral diazepam during fever 3.5%)","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"1.8-42% (drug minus placebo seizure-free or recurrence-free differential from placebo-controlled RCTs with extractable rates: vanTuijl2021 diazepam for 3 days after acute stroke 1.8%; Dreifuss1998 diazepam rectal gel acute repetitive seizure study 42%; Cereghino1998 single caregiver-administered rectal diazepam dose 21%; Autret1990 intermittent oral diazepam during fever 3.5%)","reason":"Omitted FDA-labeled 91-patient rectal diazepam gel placebo RCT result changes the maximum differential from 21% to 42%.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"vanTuijl2021|1.8|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/|784; Cereghino1998|21|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/|114; Autret1990|3.5|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2202804/|185","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"vanTuijl2021|1.8|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/|784; Dreifuss1998|42|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/|91; Cereghino1998|21|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/|114; Autret1990|3.5|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2202804/|185","reason":"Add missing 42% placebo differential from the rectal diazepam gel study summarized in FDA labeling.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9637805/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]}],"rct_link_checks":[{"label":"vanTuijl2021","pmid":"33465768","proposed_action":"Retain.","rationale":"PMID, drug, and design verified: multicenter double-blind randomized diazepam-vs-placebo EGASIS substudy in acute stroke; abstract reports seizure occurrence 1.5% vs 3.3%. Phase label not reported.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33465768/"},{"label":"Cereghino1998","pmid":"9818845","proposed_action":"Retain.","rationale":"PMID and study verified as randomized double-blind single-dose rectal diazepam formulation vs placebo for acute repetitive seizures; FDA labeling and trial report support 55% vs 34% seizure-free, differential 21%.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9818845/"},{"label":"Uhari1995","pmid":"7776115","proposed_action":"Retain but do not use for positive maximum-effect outcome.","rationale":"Randomized placebo-controlled febrile-seizure recurrence prevention trial of low intermittent diazepam/acetaminophen; available outcome evidence shows diazepam did not improve recurrence, so exclusion from positive seizure-freedom summary is appropriate.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7776115/"},{"label":"Rosman1993","pmid":"8510706","proposed_action":"Retain.","rationale":"PMID and design verified: randomized double-blind placebo-controlled oral diazepam during febrile illnesses; abstract reports reduced risk but does not provide simple randomized-arm seizure-free percentages for the CSV outcome plot.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8510706/"},{"label":"Autret1990","pmid":"2202804","proposed_action":"Retain with note that conclusion found no advantage.","rationale":"PMID and design verified: double-blind randomized oral diazepam-vs-placebo febrile-seizure recurrence trial; abstract reports recurrence 16% vs 19.5%, allowing recurrence-free conversion to 84% vs 80.5%, differential 3.5%.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2202804/"}],"source_updates":[{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; Wikipedia anticonvulsant drug-class list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata prescribing information and approval records; NCBI Bookshelf/LiverTox; Epilepsy Foundation (epilepsy.com); PubMed RCT records; ClinicalTrials.gov records","reason":"Current sources include non-trusted or disallowed sources for this audit.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547871/","https://www.epilepsy.com/treatment/seizure-rescue-therapies/oral-rescue-medicines","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00319501"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=boxed_warning_found; setid=12527569-8eb3-4624-b3c0-5c0e0c5f88c9; published=Nov 15, 2024; title=LIBERVANT (DIAZEPAM) FILM [AQUESTIVE THERAPEUTICS]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=12527569-8eb3-4624-b3c0-5c0e0c5f88c9","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata prescribing information; status=boxed_warning_found; Reference ID=5681158; title=VALTOCO (diazepam) nasal spray; url=https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","reason":"Black-box verification must use FDA/openFDA, FDA labels, or Drugs@FDA only; DailyMed is not permissible.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata labeling","reason":"Mechanism is supported directly by FDA/accessdata labels; source text should avoid DailyMed because it is outside the trusted source list supplied for this audit.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/211635Orig1s012corrected_lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/218623s000lbl.pdf"]}],"summary":"Diazepam row is partly verified but needs updates. Major issues: black-box warning source uses disallowed DailyMed; adverse-event percentages conflict with FDA label tables; formulations omit FDA-approved buccal film and include unsupported auto-injector availability; half-life text is too narrow; PubMed RCT list is incomplete; seizure-freedom maximum should be 42%, not 21%, once the missing rectal diazepam placebo RCT is included."}
